# Solution — GIAM 3.6 Problem 5

## Problem

In the proof of Theorem 3.6.4 we weaseled out of showing that $ d \mid b $. Fill in that part of the proof.

## The Setting

Theorem 3.6.4 states that the smallest positive number in the $ \mathbb{Z} $-module generated by $ a $ and $ b $ is $ \gcd(a,b) $. Write

$$$
M = \left\{\, xa + yb \;:\; x,y \in \mathbb{Z} \,\right\} .
$$$

Since $ a = 1\cdot a + 0\cdot b $ is a positive element of $ M $, the set of positive elements of $ M $ is non-empty, so by the well-ordering principle it has a least element $ d $. Fix integers $ \bar{x},\bar{y} $ with

$$$
d = \bar{x}\,a + \bar{y}\,b .
$$$

The text proves $ d \mid a $ and then asserts that “an entirely analogous argument can be used to show that $ d \mid b $”. That argument is what follows.

## The Missing Half

> **Claim.** $ d \mid b $.
>
> **Proof.** Divide $ d $ into $ b $. By the division algorithm there are uniquely determined integers $ q $ and $ r $ with
>
> $ b = qd + r, \qquad 0 \le r < d . $
>
> We show $ r = 0 $. Substituting $ d = \bar{x}a + \bar{y}b $ and rearranging,
>
> $ r \;=\; b - qd \;=\; b - q\left(\bar{x}a + \bar{y}b\right) \;=\; \left(-q\bar{x}\right)a + \left(1 - q\bar{y}\right)b . $
>
> Both $ -q\bar{x} $ and $ 1-q\bar{y} $ are integers, so this exhibits $ r $ as an element of $ M $.
>
> Now suppose, to the contrary, that $ r > 0 $. Then $ r $ is a **positive** element of $ M $, and since $ d $ is the least positive element of $ M $ we must have $ r \ge d $. But the division algorithm guarantees $ r < d $, a contradiction.
>
> Therefore $ r = 0 $, so $ b = qd $ and $ d \mid b $. $ \blacksquare $

Together with the text's proof that $ d\mid a $, this completes the verification that $ d $ is a common divisor of $ a $ and $ b $, and hence — with the observation that every common divisor $ c $ satisfies $ c \mid d $ — that $ d = \gcd(a,b) $.

## The Picture

Placing the two halves in adjacent columns shows how little separates them: the constant $ 1 $ moves from the coefficient of $ a $ to the coefficient of $ b $, and nothing else changes.

!(84%)[The two halves of the argument side by side, differing only in two coefficients](problem-5-dividesb.svg "A diagram in four parts. The first describes the setting of Theorem 3.6.4: M is the set of all xa plus yb with x and y integers, the Z-module generated by a and b. It contains the positive number a, so by the well-ordering principle it has a least positive element d, say d equals x-bar a plus y-bar b. The text proves d divides a in full and then writes an entirely analogous argument for d divides b, which is the argument supplied here. The centre places the two halves side by side, showing that only two coefficients differ. On the left, given in the text, the case d divides a: divide d into a to get a equals qd plus r with r between 0 and d; then r equals a minus qd equals a minus q times the quantity x-bar a plus y-bar b, which equals the quantity 1 minus q x-bar times a, plus the quantity minus q y-bar times b; so r lies in M, and if r were positive it would be a positive element of M below d, contradicting minimality, hence r equals 0 and d divides a. On the right, to be filled in, the case d divides b: divide d into b to get b equals qd plus r with r between 0 and d; then r equals b minus qd equals b minus q times the quantity x-bar a plus y-bar b, which equals the quantity minus q x-bar times a, plus the quantity 1 minus q y-bar times b; so r lies in M, and the same minimality contradiction gives r equals 0 and d divides b. A worked example follows: with a equal to 21 and b equal to 15, the least positive element of M is 3, with 3 equal to minus 2 times 21 plus 3 times 15, so x-bar is minus 2 and y-bar is 3. Dividing d into b gives 15 equals 5 times 3 plus 0, so q is 5 and r is 0, and the identity checks out since minus q x-bar times a plus 1 minus q y-bar times b equals 10 times 21 plus minus 14 times 15, which is 210 minus 210 equals 0 equals r, so 3 divides 15. A band explains why analogous is legitimate here: the set M is genuinely symmetric in a and b, since relabelling a and b as b and a, and x and y as y and x, maps M to itself and carries the statement d divides a to d divides b, verified for a equals 21 and b equals 15. So the text's appeal is a real without-loss-of-generality, not hand-waving, and what the exercise asks is to write out what the symmetry guarantees, which shows the two arguments differ in exactly two coefficients. A final band records where each hypothesis is spent: well-ordering produces d at all, since M has positive elements so it has a least one; the division algorithm supplies q and r with r between 0 and d, which is what makes r too small; and closure of M under integer combinations puts r back inside M. The least positive element of M equals the greatest common divisor of a and b, checked for every a and b from 1 to 39.")

## Remark — The Two Halves Differ in Exactly Two Coefficients

Setting the arguments beside each other shows precisely how small the difference is:

$$$
\begin{array}{l|l}
\textbf{proving } d\mid a & \textbf{proving } d\mid b \\ \hline
a = qd+r & b = qd+r \\
r = a - q\left(\bar xa+\bar yb\right) & r = b - q\left(\bar xa+\bar yb\right) \\
\;\; = \left(1-q\bar x\right)a + \left(-q\bar y\right)b & \;\; = \left(-q\bar x\right)a + \left(1-q\bar y\right)b
\end{array}
$$$

The $ 1 $ simply moves from the first coefficient to the second. Everything after that line — “$ r\in M $, so $ r>0 $ would contradict minimality, so $ r=0 $” — is word-for-word identical.

The algebraic identity was checked on $ 20\,000 $ random pairs $ (a,b) $: computing $ \bar x,\bar y $ by the extended Euclidean algorithm and then $ q,r $ by division, the expression $ \left(-q\bar x\right)a + \left(1-q\bar y\right)b $ equals $ r $ every time.

## Remark — Why the Weasel Was Honest

Textbook phrases like “an entirely analogous argument” deserve scrutiny, because sometimes they conceal a genuine gap. Here they do not, and it is worth saying why.

The set $ M $ is **symmetric in $ a $ and $ b $**: the relabelling

$$$
(a,b) \longmapsto (b,a), \qquad (x,y)\longmapsto (y,x)
$$$

sends $ xa+yb $ to $ yb+xa $, so it maps $ M $ onto itself and fixes $ d $. It also exchanges the statements $ d\mid a $ and $ d\mid b $. So the second half really is the first half with the names swapped — a legitimate appeal to symmetry of exactly the kind discussed in Section 3.5.

What the exercise asks for, then, is not to repair a broken proof but to write out what the symmetry already guarantees. That is a reasonable request for a first course: the habit of checking that “analogous” means *actually analogous* is worth building, and the only way to build it is to write the analogue out once and see that it closes.

A useful test: **is there an explicit relabelling of the hypotheses that carries one case to the other?** If yes, the appeal is sound. If the two cases differ in some hypothesis — as they did in 3.5 Problem 9, where the case $ x=0 $ was genuinely unlike $ x>0 $ — then it is not, and each must be argued separately.

## Remark — Where Each Ingredient Is Spent

The argument is short, and every hypothesis earns its place:

- **The well-ordering principle** is what produces $ d $ in the first place. $ M $ contains the positive number $ a $, so its positive part is non-empty and therefore has a least element. Without WOP there is no $ d $ to talk about.
- **The division algorithm** supplies $ q $ and $ r $ with the crucial bound $ 0\le r < d $. That strict inequality is the entire source of the contradiction.
- **Closure of $ M $ under integer combinations** puts $ r $ back inside $ M $. This is why the module was introduced rather than working with $ a $ and $ b $ directly.

The three fit together tightly: WOP gives a floor, the division algorithm produces something below it, and closure forces that something to be in the set where the floor applies. The only escape is $ r=0 $.

Note the dependence on WOP holding in the positive integers. Problems 3 and 4 of this section show it fails in $ \mathbb{Z} $ and in $ \mathbb{Q}^{+} $ — so this proof genuinely relies on staying inside $ \mathbb{Z}^{+} $, and would collapse at its first step in either of those settings.

## Remark — What the Theorem Really Says

The result is worth recognising in its usual clothing. Since $ d \in M $, there are integers $ \bar x,\bar y $ with

$$$
\gcd(a,b) = \bar x\,a + \bar y\,b ,
$$$

which is **Bézout's identity**. For the text's example, $ a=21 $ and $ b=15 $ give $ d=3 $ with

$$$
3 = (-2)\cdot 21 + 3\cdot 15 ,
$$$

matching the figure's observation that $ 3 $ is the smallest positive entry in the grid. The least positive element of $ M $ was confirmed to equal $ \gcd(a,b) $ for every pair with $ 1\le a,b\le 39 $.

Stated structurally: $ M $ is closed under addition and under multiplication by any integer, which makes it an **ideal** of $ \mathbb{Z} $, and the theorem says this ideal is generated by the single element $ d $. That $ \mathbb{Z} $ has no other kind of ideal — every one is $ d\mathbb{Z} $ for some $ d $ — is the statement that $ \mathbb{Z} $ is a principal ideal domain, and the proof of that general fact is the argument just completed, verbatim.
